#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/id #uuid "364c20d3-cb7d-5c14-b852-20943c6daf14" :question/math-academy-id "e-532" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/299365e1a9d092be299966bd49858b2245b86dad846e67ae2873b30ea0bd6ac9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 4x+2$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope fields at the points $(-1,-2), (0,-2),$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=4(-1)+2=-2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-2)}=4(0)+2=2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=4(0)+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/617673605e9f13423851496fcfc79d7d60bda5c0569438943ca303c812b68885.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "2b798d48-39fa-5b5c-8656-13e6a3829eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8500" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 + 2x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(-1,y)} = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = -1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point  $(-1, y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = (0)^2 + 2(0) = 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,1)} = x^2 + 2x,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x.$\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/4aece3139f017de2374529257c03c61c1b8476e6e7c2777b59d5cd2bd7897180.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5177f277-29a6-5df6-96c5-6bf53ffd9c51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a102dcd3-c75d-582e-93b9-208316a24bfb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8501" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e7aaf0356c28e989c1e694a51dae078bbe414057a95fea270968e839e9f0b7b1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope field does not vary with $y.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/60047af9610a724eca83fb1529187dee3b824fa6ff266a657843741cc1d9afd1.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we go up and down the line. \n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/d17b296f50cafc234577ebc522aa16b609df0b018b048a636402f4cfb425559f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5847d0ac-0641-5db7-bb40-0b3431d7da5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e71a4e14f96e20daff11ff8ad224d93e672cfe70b3f0302f1ed50357b1a26bb8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305868} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2d6b02-759c-542b-9515-9b49f02a9cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/74cf2bd34b