stions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b066fdee-71a9-5e00-9e2f-a971c4b9d629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165877} {:answer/id #uuid "cf50a2c6-9952-5840-a241-59279e7315d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165878} {:answer/id #uuid "3342424d-7816-502d-8c8b-264b580556de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165879} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf7026b-8693-5133-b130-82326a6abe05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165880} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce7a3a4-4220-5be7-af44-ea6805e3c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bf7026b-8693-5133-b130-82326a6abe05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165880} :answer-field/id #uuid "d49a3fd4-3209-58ee-a109-185622d0df6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eb61222-9807-5d84-aa18-34336040c466" :question/math-academy-id "q-96083" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}+2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(-2,2),$ and $(2,3).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=(2)^{2}+2(2)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/4b6ef4f2b56acf2d09a07d73c19f49f5b1c6b9eee17caa382a5c9924cbb0ea5f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488c3d7e-972e-5d22-896b-e1332f1f6c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a8c3e6-d4b4-5eaf-852b-73cfaa32e21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305918} {:answer/id #uuid "ba089040-a864-53ad-b8f3-b111ca8d6854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305919} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1e9b05-7f6e-5513-b90b-a76506b6c628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305920} {:answer/id #uuid "19156c10-3d31-50d6-9bda-6858c5c4f4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "488c3d7e-972e-5d22-896b-e1332f1f6c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} :answer-field/id #uuid "55462a1c-8e1a-54ba-86d8-3bc4a1241abe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26b5af41-aa8e-538e-8148-814f5268dc52" :question/math-academy-id "q-96457" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x-2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $x=2,$ the slope field is horizontal" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=-2-2=-4<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-2=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,y)}=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(2,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/34ab84e5a2b05adad50384cbbb33f48233976aa9e757a40468d5778709801d83.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e1fbd3-d5f5-5631-8c5e-851b88a50197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305924} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5b7e5b-32a5-5635-94a6-36edcd4d91e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305925} {:answer/id #uuid "3e65d89f-ad53-5995-869e-d9e804392388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305926} {:answer/id #uuid "47f181dd-3a48-5346-8d97-13e0eb37932a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305927} {:answer/id #uuid "3ade0966-1801-597b-a5ec-cf6aaddffe25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e1fbd3-d5f5-5631-8c5e-851b88a50197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305924} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafc1843-6256-5c96-a316-ceaa9cda3fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2deae67f-b66c-5463-b4cc-a6e8cdf95fcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96460" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=(-1)^{3}+(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=(0)^{2}+0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=(1)^{3}+1=2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/45427ef9ff00752ad528335ae51143c27fcc01d9c535d2fe380edfbbfdb9b167.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the 